  "reserves": [
    "This Keg is one operator's substrate declaration. It is not a template. It is not a requirement. It is not a claim about how every grove gets composed. The canon (GRV-004, Bicameral Canon) holds without this Keg.",
    "Memoir register is provisional — proposed here as a seventh entry in the canon's register inventory, to be formally adopted or declined in Bicameral Canon v1.1.",
    "Lineage links are operator-asserted, not Grove-verified. Predecessors are named from the operator's memory of their actual contribution to his substrate, not from archival research.",
    "The Keg is offered because the operator believes declaring substrate honestly is itself a grounding move under the protocol; if that belief is wrong, the reader is invited to say so.",
    "This work is fundamentally multicameral. Two or more chambers — human, machine, surface, operator — sharing ground via protocol become one circuit. Composing happens by declaration and instantiation across that circuit, not inside any single chamber. The personal Keg is one chamber. It sits inert. The composing LLM is another. It sits inert. The registrar is another. It sits inert. The reader's own substrate is another (operator or reader, your choice of perspective). Coherence emerges across them when the protocol holds, not because any single chamber exists in the void. The medium is the message. McLuhan was right. Vonnegut knew. Synesthesia is the human-scale proof.",
    "Per-recipient substrate (Option C) — forthcoming. Recipients named in this letter's dedication will receive per-recipient Keg surfaces accessible through Andon clearance at /substrate/jim-calhoun/letter/andon/. Demonstrates the multi-register claim structurally."
  ],
